{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792493362,"nonce":"z8xXNsCMRi1D0dyrdvjAcuRDi3w=","title":"Dead Int-keyed Maps keep their native storage: footprint does not fall after they are collected","message":"A Haxe Map keyed by Int keeps its table in malloc memory (hlp_hiset, hashbrown). After 60,000 such maps are dropped and collected, the Haxe heap goes back to 0 MB but the process footprint stays where it was, and malloc_zone_pressure_relief returns nothing. A Map keyed by String gives its memory back in the same test.\n\n**Reproducer**\n\n/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-amaterasu-Vibranium-ashui/ab292b42-0b79-454c-aa0c-2c901f96c1a1/scratchpad/mapfree/MapFree.hx. It makes N maps of 8 entries, drops them, calls hl.Gc.major() twice and sleeps, for a number of cycles, and prints the Haxe heap beside each mark. Build `ash --build mapfree-aot mapfree.hl` and run `./mapfree-aot 60000 \u003ckind\u003e \u003ccycles\u003e`; kind 0 is Map\u003cInt, Bool\u003e, 1 is Map\u003cInt, Int64\u003e, 2 is Map\u003cString, Bool\u003e. The sibling run.sh samples `footprint -p` at each MARK line. Ash built from 6bcf617c, AOT and hybrid alike.\n\n**What it shows (footprint, MB)**\n\n- Map\u003cInt, Bool\u003e, one cycle: 3.5 at start, 30 with the maps, 30 after drop and two majors, with the Haxe heap at 0.\n- Map\u003cInt, Int64\u003e, one cycle: 53 to 53 after the drop.\n- Six cycles of Map\u003cInt, Bool\u003e: 53, 77, 121, 137, 146, 146, 146. It stops growing after about four cycles, so the storage is reused or freed late, but it is not given back.\n- Map\u003cString, Bool\u003e, six cycles: 103 with the maps, then back to 15.\n- With the six cycles done and every map dead, vmmap shows MALLOC_SMALL 140 MB, and malloc_zone_pressure_relief(NULL, 0) returns 0 and leaves the footprint at 145 to 148 MB.\n\n**Where it shows in an app**\n\nRayDE (ashui) opens 30 files and closes them all, AOT build: 67 MB over a fresh window afterwards. A malloc_history call tree at startup and after the close shows about 24,500 live hlp_hiset blocks (+5.0 MB) and about 25,000 live hlp_hi64alloc blocks (+2.4 MB), allocated from ashui's Node.style, Node.styleAll and Node.set, which each make small int maps. ashui's registries are back to their startup size, so these are dead maps, not live ones.\n\n**Question**\n\nIs the table freed by a finalizer, and when does it run? If it is freed late, is a pass at idle that frees it, or a purge of the malloc zone afterwards, in scope? A dead IntMap costs about 430 bytes here, and a UI makes thousands of them.","files":null}]}